  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nThe remnants of hurricane Polo have merged with a deep trough over \nthe western US and a cut-off low is not prominent over Colorado and \nNew Mexico. At the surface, a cold front is developing associated \nwith this system and will push eastward over the next couple of \ndays. There is still considerable uncertainty in how exactly the \nrain plays out and how quickly the front moves through. The \nprobability of a morning round of convection appears to be \ndecreasing based on current radar/satellite trends and how models \nare initializing.  Most high res models have either given up on the \nmorning line completely or at least backed off on how much \nconvection there will be. That said, will keep mainly low end (20-\n30%) pops for the morning with the exception of western parts of \nWebb and LaSalle county where things could start ramping up a bit \nearlier. The best potential for convection will be in the afternoon \nand overnight hours today and tonight with daytime heating and the \nmid-level low/trough moving closer. Regardless of when and where the \nrain occurs, with PWAT values nearly off the charts, the rain will \nfall very fast and heavy when it falls.  PWAT value on the 18Z CRP \nsounding was 2.41\", down a bit to 2.28\" at the 00Z sounding.  These \nare well above the 90th percentile in climatology and around the \ndaily Max values. CRP almost certainly tops the current max value \nfor tomorrow which is an outlier low record of 2.09\". These high \nmoisture levels are capable of producing rainfall rates 2-3\"/hr, \nwith isolated cells producing even higher rates. Probabilities of 48 \nhour rainfall through Friday over 3\" are moderate to high over the \nwestern portions of the area, but not out of the question east with \nany given slow-moving storm capable of dumping that much. \n\nThe forecast struggle does not end after the Thursday/Thursday night \nrains, as the convergence line (stalled boundary) is likely to set \nup near the coast and keep rain chances going through the weekend, \npotentially into early next week, but this would be mainly along the \nimmediate coast and into the marine areas. We also will see a couple \nof tropical waves move through the Gulf further enhancing moisture. \nBottom line, it's going to rain...potentially a lot...and the \nforecast is still pretty tricky. \n\nTaking a moment to talk temperatures, today is tricky...but with the \nlower probability of a morning round of convection, temps will \nlikely once again be in the mid and upper 90s for most of the area. \nIf convection begins earlier, this could certainly fall with some \nareas not getting out of the 80s.  Speaking of 80s...it looks like a \nlot of highs in the 80s for the foreseeable future, finally getting \nback to normal or slightly below.",
